  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Friday)...\n\nWater vapor loop shows two distinct short waves moving across the\nregion overnight. One, which produced a line of intense lightning\nand rainfall across our east earlier last evening, is exiting\nMontana into the Dakotas at this time. The second is sweeping\nacross our CWA at this time producing scattered showers and\nisolated thunderstorms. Locally brief heavy rain is still possible\nwith some of this activity with a slight chance of some small \nhail. These will likely linger across our eastern counties \nthrough about 4-5 AM before dissipating or tracking into the \nDakotas. \n\nIndependence Day is expected to be warmer and dry, with highs in \nthe upper 80s to lower 90s. There is just a very low chance \n(<15%) for a few showers developing over the Beartooths/Absarokas \nin the evening, though this may turn out to be just a few\nsprinkles or virga. Sunday looks to be the hottest day of the \nnext several as ridging builds over eastern MT. High temperatures \nare forecast to range from the middle 80s to the upper 90s, with \nsome of the hotter valley locations potentially nearing 100 \ndegrees. By the late afternoon and evening, a weak shortwave \nlifting into the region from the southwest will bring a chance  \n(20-40%) for showers and thunderstorms, spreading east into the \novernight hours. The risk of any severe storm is low (<10%) with\nthis activity. \n\nA low amplitude upper ridge will generally prevail over our \nregion for the work week ahead. High temps will be on the warm\nside from 80s to lower 90s. Ensembles do hint at periodic short\nwaves in the mean mid level flow bringing a chance of late day\nshowers and thunderstorms each day...with the best chance of\nprecipitation Monday through Wednesday (30-50%). The chance of\nrain becomes more isolated across the east Thursday/Friday as\nwesterly flow pushes low level moisture east. While the threat \nfor severe thunderstorms is currently low, will need to keep an \neye on the potential. BT",
